abstract: >-
  Off-policy evaluation (OPE) is used to decide whether a treatment policy learned from hospital
  records deserves a prospective trial, and every standard estimator assumes that the records
  contain everything the clinicians acted on. This report studies what happens when they do not,
  on the sepsis simulator of Oberst and Sontag (2019), extended with a hidden variable whose
  strength is set exactly. Because the simulator is ported to an exact transition tensor, the
  true value of every policy, the confounding strength Γ* under the marginal sensitivity model,
  and the infinite-data limit of every estimator are computed rather than estimated. The
  strength of confounding alone does not determine whether OPE recommends a worse policy. With
  infinite data, the estimators prefer withholding antibiotics, which is truly worse than the
  clinicians' policy, from Γ* = 12 when the hidden variable also worsens outcomes directly, and
  at no Γ* up to 152 when it harms only untreated patients. With 10,000 episodes, weighted
  importance sampling and a model-based estimate make this recommendation in 84% and 70% of
  datasets at Γ* = 12. With 2,000 episodes or fewer, the finite-sample biases of these two
  estimators, which have opposite signs, are as large as the confounding bias. Sharp sensitivity
  bounds at the true Γ* cover the true value in 96% to 99.9% of confounded datasets with 10,000
  episodes, but a breakdown-point alarm calibrated on recorded variables fires on 86% to 100% of
  deploy decisions, with a precision no better than the base rate of wrong decisions.
